The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". Its own cause table
disagrees, and so do the desk's notes. Read by tenant and by body, the reports in #4471 are
three different bugs. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships. Two of its problems have no fix queued.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant here writes
  the day first. Day and month come out swapped. This is the bug #4471 names.
- **`hint`**: the `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the
  date the tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint says. A parser fix does not reach it.
- **`one-day`**: the booking slides by one day, a weekday earlier ("Client in Lisbon got
  Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday."). No swap at all: a time-zone bug. The desk already
  read these reports this way (`desk-notes.json`).

## How it was counted

With the desk's own view, as exported (`#lib/kb/causes.ts`):

1. One report per id: the export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
2. Cause by tenant, not by intake tag: `meta.intakeTag` is a guess from the reporter's
   browser, and it disagrees with the tenant (compare *tagged as* above).
3. A report stays counted where it is filed. The reports the desk read as a one-day move are
   filed under `parser` by tenant and counted there; they get their own row and ticket too.

The logic is in `#lib/kb/problems.ts`, on top of `#lib/kb/causes.ts`.

## What the cause table shows

The same reports through the kb's existing view, for comparison:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A subject line says how a report sounds, not what broke. Merging on subject
  ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) hid two bugs inside one ticket.
- Count from the data, once per id, and place reports by tenant. Intake tags are guesses.

